These endpoint snapshots and counters show differences, not their full causal
order. The exact schema-v13 fault-event replay and schema-v14 pre-copy
stack-copy capture are from these same source runs. The v14 snapshot locally
explains the extra run-2 `#PF`: run 1's seven-byte copy destination shares the
mapped page with `bprm_p`; in run 2, stack alignment puts the destination on an
adjacent lower page whose PT leaf is absent. The upstream reason for the
different aligned stack position/page state remains unknown. The exact v13
fault-event replay for these same source runs
records 33 and 34 delivered `#PF` events, respectively, with no omissions.
Run 2 has one inserted event after the first 30 common tuples: a CPL0 write
fault at `rep_movs_alternative+0x11` while populating the initial `/init`
stack. This additional `#PF` numerically accounts for the +1 page-fault and
aggregate delivered-exception counts (each run also delivered one `#GP(0)`).
Together, v13 and v14 identify the local cause of the extra fault and its
additional exception. They do not connect that fault to the +1 `cr3_changes`
counter.

RDTSC accounting and terminal-loop evidence
-------------------------------------------
The full 22-site RDTSC histograms reconcile to the source totals. Delay sites
account for +10,402 poll reads and +11 entry reads, or +10,413 combined. Other
sites contribute the remaining net +60. Per-call summaries attribute all +11
delay entries to `vpanic+0x497` through `__const_udelay`; the completed-call
and final-partial-poll balance is recorded in
`s5-07-delay-call-summary-exact-replay-20261117.log`. The descriptive median
of 975 polls per vpanic call is not a fixed-cost guarantee. The +60 at other
RDTSC sites and the +1 CR3 counter are not explained by that caller/poll
balance, and the delay-count increase is not causally linked to the extra
page fault.

Actual first UART difference
----------------------------
The strict comparator applies nonce substitution only. The first differing
byte is offset 6,569 (zero-based), on UART line 96 (one-based):

* Run 0001: `[    0.021286] KHO: Failed to reserve scratch area, disabling kexec handover`
* Run 0002: `[    0.021285] KHO: Failed to reserve scratch area, disabling kexec handover`

The nearest common preceding line is line 95, `[    0.021252] KHO: Failed to
reserve lowmem scratch buffer`. This is an observed one-microsecond timestamp
difference on the same message; no timing normalization was active because the
eligibility gate failed. Later UART differences include the terminal panic
stack. Its normalizer is also disabled by unknown optional frames; run 1 first
has `? psi_task_switch+0x12a/0x390` at original UART line 328, while run 2
first has `? schedule+0x27/0xb0` at original UART line 331. A manually forced
Stage4 panic projection has a common `do_exit.cold+0x15/0x15` anchor, but that
projection is not the comparator result and must not be used to claim the
strict UART first difference.

Conclusion and next step
------------------------
The corrected offline ledger records the actual ineligible comparator path,
all 26 machine stop leaf differences, and the first nonce-normalized UART
byte. Earlier five-field/`psi_task_switch` findings came from a manually
forced projection that the strict comparator does not use. The v13/v14
captures already localize the extra `#PF`; no further replay is needed for
that bounded explanation. S5-07 remains blocked/unaccepted; the comparator
and allowlist remain unchanged.

The existing schema-v14 capture is from this exact pair and confirms the
local stack-copy fault mechanism, but it does not record invocation-local
`cr3_changes`, RDTSC or retired deltas. If pursuing the remaining +1 CR3
counter, the next diagnostic slice can bound a trace to the identified
`create_elf_tables` platform-string copy invocation, from copy-site entry
through fault/return, and record those local deltas plus PF/exception counts.
Mark a capped capture incomplete. This would not explain later terminal-loop
variation or make the series pass.

